  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 347 AM CDT Tue Sep 15 2026\n\n     TODAY THROUGH THURSDAY\n\nEarly this morning, upper air analysis shows a sharp, potent \nshortwave trough exiting the Upper Midwest with southwesterly flow \nleft in it's wake. A weak upper wave was noted across the southern \nRockies embedded within this southwesterly flow aloft slowly\nprogressing northeastward. At the surface, a sharp cold front \nis located over southern Nebraska and far northern Kansas\nlumbering its way southward. Both of the aforementioned \nfeatures will be the catalyst for scattered showers and storms \nlater today and tonight across much forecast area. \n\nA few showers are occurring across northern and central Kansas early \nthis morning thanks to strong mid-level WAA overspreading these \nareas. As the LLJ weakens later this morning, WAA will wane, but \nsurface convergence will increase as low-level CAA ramps up and\nbegins to push the cold front southward. During this, widely \nscattered showers and storms are possible across portions of \ncentral Kansas this morning into early this afternoon. \nEventually, the surface front will sag its way to within \nvicinity of the Kansas Turnpike by this afternoon. While the \nfront is sinking southward, strong diurnal heating is \nanticipated ahead of it, and the front is likely to interact \nwith a very unstable airmass. MLCAPE around 1500-2500 J/kg will \nsupport some strong to marginally severe storms capable of \ndownbursts producing damaging wind gusts. Some of the latest \nshort-term guidance shows DCAPE over 1500 J/kg across a large \nportion of the area along the cold front. Model soundings back \nthis up with a classic inverted-v profile indicative of an \nenvironment favorable for microbursts. The main limiting factor \nto the intensity of storms today will be the lack of shear. \nEffective shear will be in the neighborhood of 20-25 knots, so \nstorm lifetimes will be short, likely producing one or two \nstrong downbursts before weakening/dissipating. In addition to \nthe possibility of damaging wind gusts today, PWATs in the \nneighborhood of 1.7-1.9 inches will support heavy rainfall. \nStorms are expected to train along the cold front today into \ntonight, and multiple bouts of very heavy rainfall could lead to\nlocalized flooding in some locations, even with (somewhat) dry \nantecedent conditions. The cold front begins to wash out by \ntonight, and the main focus for thunderstorm activity will \ntransition to the mid-level baroclinic zone which will be placed\nin the vicinity of I-70 after dark. As forcing for ascent \ngradually moves northward, so will storm chances late tonight \nthrough Wednesday morning.\n\nUnfortunately, this cold front will not bring a cool down for most \nlocations across the forecast area, and for those who do see it \n(locations along the I-70 corridor), it will only be temporary as \nwarmer air surges northward on Wednesday. Supported by a \nstrengthening mid/upper ridge, afternoon highs will rise to the mid \nto upper 90s across southern and eastern Kansas while portions of \ncentral Kansas rise into the mid to upper 80s. Warmer temperatures \nspread even further north with mid to upper 90s overspreading \nall of the forecast area Thursday afternoon as the \naforementioned mid/upper ridge continues to strengthen over \nportions of the central plains.\n\n     FRIDAY THROUGH MONDAY\n\nThe mid/upper ridge responsible for the mid-week heat will weaken \nrather quickly as it get squeezed between a retrograding upper low \nover the Gulf coastline and the region's next system arriving \nout from over the western CONUS. Still, it won't weaken enough \nto prevent one last very warm day across the region on Friday with\nhighs in the mid to upper 90s. At this time, a cold front is \nforecast to arrive by Friday night, and will slowly lumber its \nway across the area throughout the weekend. Currently, mid/long-\nrange guidance seems to be much more confident that this will \nbring some much more fall-like air to the region. However, it's \nstill 5-7 days out, so this could be subject to change; though \nit should be noted that both the GEFS and ECMWF ensembles show\nnot that much spread in temperatures in this time frame. In \naddition to the possible cooler temperatures, more chances for \nshowers and storms are also currently being forecast for this \nweekend for most of the forecast area.",
